ngth of the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $-\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ (shown in blue in the  diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+1+3{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+3{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0b4da9-1a0f-5a46-aff1-3ce5c0cebb3b" :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e0e74e8-2eb3-5075-b7fe-5a7ec0e1a5f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354594} {:answer/id #uuid "b35d0f14-45d4-5efa-873f-b135d56081c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354595} {:answer/id #uuid "e63c7b44-4ef5-5105-9191-12eafdd161f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354596} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee3a983-e1bd-5b23-bd2a-cedd5a2abb0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354597} {:answer/id #uuid "588b97c3-1345-582c-929b-e21267bbf352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e0e74e8-2eb3-5075-b7fe-5a7ec0e1a5f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354594} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a8b5ba9-6b37-5e65-9531-a58530a68304" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a434871-4fea-52cd-80a4-1fd81c159fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-15133" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/3a760f55b6437a39010e4d1a00bdb5fb5390d848dd74347e7736e2ac49cd562b.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}+(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+144{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta ))}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84a533e8-9a7c-58f3-9d3c-7c08b3c6bfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4db64b8-39e3-5c3b-bc74-edf065084c81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-{e}^{-\\pi /4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354601} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf02204-23e8-5e54-a4dd-0835514589fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354602} {:answer/id #uuid "f2cc63ef-146d-5d35-9c03-aa861095956e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354603} {:answer/id #uuid "7de1217e-7df7-5c94-8bd2-95e6bd28e197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{\\pi /4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354604} {:answer/id #uuid "64de7071-e0e2-52b9-a70e-48a7c56e596b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2cc63ef-146d-5d35-9c03-aa861095956e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354603} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5b447a4-4384-55a0-831a-bdb7fbf86d1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eeba4fa8-124a-54c9-ba6c-4f189488c740" :question/math-academy-id "q-20070" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/fa1c1c3ae2769f45854de4f0b00fc394fccb40b40704cfd09e6eb11eee3c07af.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=3{e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(3{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{(3{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{e}^{2\\theta }+9{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{18{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84a533e8-9a7c-58f3-9d3c-7c08b3c6bfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d05a954-59b2-5a95-8e0d-489514000361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(e^{\\pi}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d05a954-59b2-5a95-8e0d-489514000361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(e^{\\pi}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354608} :answer-field/id #uuid "6facb5aa-f66d-5704-b851-c125a1e4ce3a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11886f6e-15f4-57af-a1dd-d57334eb5acd" :question/math-academy-id "q-353598" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/16b676776dc22aece5a1fa777007a03b9e7903706cbdf5cc4ae14d8508370419.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r={e}^{2\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{2\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2{e}^{2\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}+{(2{e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4\\theta }+4{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{[{e}^{2\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{2(\\pi /2)}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84a533e8-9a7c-58f3-9d3c-7c08b3c6bfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6064233-ed38-5ef3-8349-1dd3a03c75b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091680} {:answer/id #uuid "db306417-a82a-5664-b0d9-2362a93d761e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091681} {:answer/id #uuid "0f1e274f-8eb5-5481-b78c-b5fbba99795e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091682} {:answer/id #uuid "6c96387a-f2f3-5c59-a3a3-92a83acc5088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091683} {:answer/id #uuid "74a7db6a-2d70-5ff8-b64d-297fa28f56d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db306417-a82a-5664-b0d9-2362a93d761e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091681} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1c56fba-9bc3-51b4-a438-d20c93c6b9e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dfaa6223-b113-5398-adeb-e8d8b6d36965" :question/math-academy-id "q-9532" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/085be1e624ae99afee256d2a9591f17d2f33f688364b6e59fdcd7c415206bda7.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}1\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta {|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
